1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:01,800
In this video, I'm going to run through 14

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,400
different types of aircraft to tell

3
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:04,920
the story of how we learned to fly.

4
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,170
So let's go through them one by one

5
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:07,520
and look at how they were designed.

6
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:08,940
Starting off, we have the glider.

7
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:10,730
Before anyone put an engine on a plane,

8
00:00:10,730 --> 00:00:12,260
someone had to prove that a wing

9
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,640
could actually hold a person up.

10
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:15,480
And that person was Otto Lilienthal,

11
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:17,280
a German engineer in the 1890s.

12
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,330
He built his own gliders, which were big,

13
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:20,800
curved wings made of fabric,

14
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:21,600
kind of like bat wings.

15
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,390
And he would hold onto the glider, run off

16
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:24,680
the top of a hill and glide down.

17
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:26,750
And he did this over and over, about 2,000

18
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:27,520
times in total.

19
00:00:27,550 --> 00:00:29,360
He even had a hill built for this exact

20
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:31,680
purpose, which is about 15 meters tall,

21
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:32,900
just so he could launch into the wind

22
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:33,760
from any direction.

23
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:36,200
His longest glides went about 250 meters.

24
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,990
So his gliders had pretty much no

25
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:39,080
controls at all.

26
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,960
So he wasn't really able to steer, besides

27
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:42,290
when he swung his legs around and

28
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:43,080
moved his body around.

29
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:44,770
That shifted the weight and the glider

30
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:46,280
would tilt in that direction.

31
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,310
And it's basically how a hang

32
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:48,200
glider works today.

33
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:49,920
He also wrote down everything he learned.

34
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:51,530
And one of his biggest findings

35
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:53,240
was that a curved wing makes much

36
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:54,720
more lift than a flat one.

37
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:56,180
And that curve is called camber,

38
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:57,880
and almost every wing today has it.

39
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:59,860
He published tables of how much lift

40
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:02,120
a curved wing makes at different angles.

41
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,540
And that gave the people after him

42
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:04,680
something to build on.

43
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:06,480
But obviously, steering with your body

44
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,080
only works up to a certain point.

45
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:09,780
The design problem is that as you make

46
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:11,780
the wing bigger, the forces on it grow

47
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:13,480
much faster than your ability to move

48
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:14,440
your weight around.

49
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:15,640
So a bigger glider can carry

50
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:17,260
more, but it's also just much harder

51
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:18,000
to control.

52
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,740
In 1896, Lilienthal lost control, fell

53
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,010
about 15 meters, he broke his spine,

54
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:24,200
and he died the next day.

55
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:25,430
So today, these gliders are

56
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:26,480
called sailplanes.

57
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,540
They have really long, skinny wings,

58
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:30,440
some over 25 meters across.

59
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,970
And this shape basically means that every

60
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:34,720
wing leaks air around its tips,

61
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:37,320
which creates swirls called tip vortices.

62
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:38,920
And these swirls cause a bit of drag.

63
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:41,100
Basically, a long, narrow wing has less

64
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,200
tip compared to the rest of the wing.

65
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:44,840
So it wastes a lot less energy.

66
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,980
And engineers will call this like a high

67
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:47,960
aspect ratio.

68
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:50,250
So pilots are able to find pockets of warm

69
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:51,920
air that rise off the ground

70
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:52,840
and they ride them upwards.

71
00:01:52,870 --> 00:01:54,450
And that means that they can stay up

72
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:56,370
for hours without an engine and the best

73
00:01:56,370 --> 00:01:58,267
sailplanes travel more than 50 meters

74
00:01:58,267 --> 00:01:59,920
forward for every meter that they drop.

75
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,450
There's one pilot that has flown over 3000

76
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:03,870
kilometers in a single flight

77
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:06,180
and a special research glider has climbed

78
00:02:06,180 --> 00:02:08,200
above 23000 meters.

79
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:09,990
So gliders basically proved that flying

80
00:02:09,990 --> 00:02:11,380
was possible, but they had two

81
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:12,080
big problems.

82
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,990
There was no proper way to steer and there

83
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:15,840
was no engine to keep them in the air.

